d led  the first thing I saw when I walked in  the door was this giant portrait of Ed  Ziegler  people there spoke of him with such  reverence and over the years I became  increasingly aware of the admiration and  appreciation people had Fred everywhere  but especially among child Advocates and  practitioners  Ed was a scholar and he advised his  students to be Scholars first if they  wanted to have an impact to earn not to  expect attention from policy makers  but at heart he was an advocate for  children especially children who were  overlooked and underserved  that heartfelt commitment to improving  children's lives is what people  experienced and revered him for  he was also a Savvy and extraordinarily  effective he was willing to collaborate  with people from every political side to  find common ground this is as we all  know a rare quality these days  she persisted and regrouped when  necessary reminding us all that we might  not be successful in a particular moment  but we need to be ready when the right  moment came along  Ed taught us to remain Vigilant after  victories because successes can be  easily reversed he had a vision that his  students and everybody who worked with  him  needed to be the very very best they  could be and that it was his job to  inspire that bestness in all of us if I  was working on a chapter or working on a  journal article with Ed I felt like the  weight of all the children of the world  was was on my shoulders with this  article that that the lives of babies  were in the balance on the basis of just  some article that I was writing and I  think it was really because he he  carried that kind of sense of urgency  too if you called him up you better be  prepared for the answer because he was  going to give it to you straight and I  appreciated that about him if I was off  on something he didn't hesitate to tell  me he would edit with this big red pen  that you would get it back and you would  think oh my gosh am I going to possibly  survive this my office was uh in many  ways a home to many of students after  their encounters with that so yes it is  true he was hard but it was always in  everybody's best interests and we all  strive to live up to his expectations  for all of us and frankly I think he  made every one of us better Scholars as  a result of that Ed turned me into a  scholar first and foremost whose  activism Drew from science  so what was it like being ed student  it could be terrifying at times he did  not tolerate fools or students who gave  up on themselves but he always had his  students backs he always picked us up  when we faltered and he always  celebrated our achievements he was like  a second father to me that is the kind  of person who will tell you what he  thinks to your face and defend you  behind your back if you didn't know  where you stood with Ed Ziggler you  simply were not paying attention he even  made it perfectly clear where things  stood but you also knew exactly why he  was that way because he took it so  seriously and as a result it was  impossible to not take it seriously with  him I remember Larry Aver telling me  before I even applied to Yale  that working with Ed would give me a  tough exact thing  irascible advisor  but one who would remain steadfast and  loyal to me for the rest of my career  and Larry was so right  Ed was with me at every step writing  letters of recommendation  being a cheerleader telling me I could  do it helping me to reflect on things  and pushing me to take the next step  in my second year of graduate school  family tragedy befell my extended family  and I thought I would need to grow up  out of graduate school to fulfill my  responsibilities to my extended family  Ed to ask Larry do you want to drop out  of graduate school and I said no  he said then let's find a way  and he promptly brainstormed with me  how to keep me in graduate school for me  remembering Ed now is more about the  ways in which he welcomed encouraged and  supported me and all of his other  graduate students into the world of what  I will call provocative scholarship  inextricably linked with impassioned  action  the admiration and respect that AP and  the psychology Community have for Ed are  beyond measure Dr Robert Sternberg  another former APA president awarded a  presidential citation to add in 2003.  that same year it was recognized with a  mentor award in developmental psychology  and he previously received the AP award  for distinguished contributions to  Therian research in community psychology  yet I understand that Ed was the most  proud of receiving apa's Premier award  for outstanding lifetime contributions  to psychology in 2008. I will never ever  forget my first meeting with Dr Ziegler  when we were talking to him about the  idea that grew into jumpstart the  Americorps program that trains college  students to work one-on-one with  preschoolers of course we thought he  hated the idea and was completely  unhappy and about to throw us out of his  office based on his demeanor and how he  was interacting with this while we were  talking and then at the end he said I  like it and I'll give you a thousand  dollars to help get this idea off the  ground it completely changed everything  for us he didn't just give us the  thousand dollars from the old Bush  Center for Child Development he also  gave us an office right around the  corner from his office which was the  first jumpstart office ever what started  in that office has now grown into truly  a national movement over 60 000 college  students have served as jumpstart Corps  members they've worked with over a  hundred and twenty thousand preschoolers  all across the United States and I'm not  sure that any of that would have  happened without Dr Ziegler giving us  that first thousand bucks and believing  in us and believing in young people  which is one of the many amazing things  about Dr Ziegler's entire career I was  very early in my career and participated  in a conference in Burlington Vermont in  which it was the keynote of course as  always and I had some very minor role  and  um at some point during the day there  was a press conference being held and Ed  was going to meet with the local  reporters and invited me to come and  participate and I said oh my God I no  way I'm too intimidated I'm afraid of  the press I'm afraid that I'll be  misquoted and he said he looked me in  the eye and he said let me tell you  something  um if you want to kind of play in this  Arena and want to have an influence you  have to put yourself out there and be  willing to speak to the Press  do your best at putting out there what  you want to say but remember never  forget that once you say something you  have no control over what people do with  it  so you give it your best shot you don't  expect to have any say about what  happens after that  um and if you can't handle that kind of  pressure then don't expect that you're  going to be able to make a difference in  that Arena I never remember Ed wanting  to be on the camera wanting to be  covered wanting to be quoted except if  he thought it would assist and help the  issue it embody for me how one can  utilize academic training to  continuously try to make the world a  better place for everyone  I look back on my career post Yale and  recognized that it was not by accident  that those 35 some odd years at the  Hartford foundation for public giving  were centered around the development of  policies and programs to support young  children and their families  moreover on a very personal level my  career as a father has been shaped by  his teachings questioning and  encouragement to deeply understand and  engage in this remarkable life event  thank you so very much Ed for helping to  steer me onto these incredibly important  Paths of learning of being and of doing  it's a real honor and pleasure to have  the opportunity to highlight Ed's impact  on my life well I was honored to bring  together 23 Scholars practitioners and  Advocates to craft an article to honor  Ed entitled Pathways to a more peaceful  and sustainable world  the article provides an overview of  selected ongoing International efforts  that have been inspired by Ed's Vision  to improve programs and policies for  Young Children and Families  the efforts presented are in close  alignment with strategies articulated by  Ed several years later we found out that  red was going to need surgery and Dr Kim  the anesthesiologist went over to Dr  Zucker and said I was a head start  graduate my mother said that the only  reason that I turned out as well off as  I did was because I had such a good  start to education from the Head Start  experience that I had after the surgery  was over it really wasn't the kind of  follow-up that sounded like a doctor  checking in on a patient it was more  like a man checking in on his previously  unknown benefactor somebody that he felt  had really helped change his life  [Music]  the numbers of people  the numbers of policies  and the numbers of visionary steps  that he architected  LED this nation  to a far better however still imperfect  but far better agenda for Children and  Families he was brilliant he was at The  Cutting Edge of everything and and a  Visionary in every way  so we all have a lot to appreciate and  be thankful for  50 years ago Sergeant Shriver one of the  architects of the war on poverty  hand-picked a brilliant young academic  from Yale University Edward Ziegler to  serve as a leading expert tasked with  designing the program that became Head  Start  President Johnson spoke of this project  as in I quote a new war front on poverty  he said and again quote we set out to  make certain that poverty's children  would not be forevermore poverty's  captives  Professor Ziegler soon became known as  the father of Head Start  and with good reason  and even today  it is difficult to overstate how  audacious how revolutionary Dr Ziegler's  Vision really is  Head Start brings together everything  everything that matters most to young  children education health emotional  well-being nutrition Social Services  instead of treating children as isolated  individuals Head Start Embraces whole  families involving parents and making  sure their kids are ready to learn  Head Start is without question the most  effective early childhood development  program that our country has ever  developed  his gifts to the United States  do not stop with Head Start  he helped give our country family a  medical leave assistance with child care  zero to three  and with Dr Jim Comer the Cozy  initiative the Yale child study center  but his greatest Legacy  is the Stars  too numerous to name who have been  trained at Yale  under his tutelage Ed left an indelible  mark on our community and our nation it  was my great privilege to not only have  had the opportunity to work closely with  him but to have called him my friend and  today it is my great honor to join all  of you in Remembering as invaluable  contributions not only to his chosen  profession but to all of our Lives May  his spirit and vision continue to be a  guide to us all when I'm asked what am I  most proud of as a senator an offered a  lot of legislation I come back to  children every single time other things  may have had a larger impact at the  moment and so forth but nothing I did  will have the lasting impact that the  cluster of issues around children and  their families did Ned ziglet was the  author of an awful lot of those ideas  and how to get them done he wanted us to  always do better he wanted our country  to do better he wanted Scholars to do  better by by our country he wanted those  of us who were in The Profession  teachers and school administrators he  wanted us to do better because he knew  we could and he knew there was a better  future for children if only we dedicated  ourselves more fully to it upon  receiving one of his numerous Awards Ed  reflected on the Head Start program he  conceptualized he said if I had never  done anything else in my life that would  have been a life worth living  but of course Head Start was just one  way Ed improved our world  he advised every U.S president from  Lyndon Johnson to Barack Obama on child  and family policy he was a prolific  scholar who published over 40 books and  800 papers and he was pivotal to the  Family and Medical Leave acts Passage  throughout his life Ed brought much  honor to yell  an equal measure of Hope to millions of  youths across America  as we pay tribute to our devoted  colleague  let us find solace in the fact that Ed  Ziggler's Legacy endures through the  many lives he enriched  [Music]
 

